sections.body:
Let's start with the program that falls behind. Kafka keeps records in topics, which are named logs, and splits each topic into partitions, separate logs that each keep their own order. The texting program, which the code calls the notification program, reads the `notifications` topic, which has 6 partitions. For each payment it sends the customer a text message, an SMS, and each send takes about 200 ms, because the company that delivers the texts is slow.

The program runs as a consumer group: several copies of it, called members, that share the reading. Consumer groups and rebalancing set them up. In a consumer group, each partition has exactly one owner, like each rail and its one cook. So the group can use at most 6 members, and a seventh sits idle. Six members at 5 sends a second each make 30 texts a second. On a sale day, payments reach 300 a second, and the backlog grows by 270 every second.

Each consumer-group fix hurts. More partitions means 60 of them for 60 members. They'd have to be chosen up front, because adding partitions later moves keys to different partitions (Producers I: keys, partitions and ordering). And most of the year they'd sit nearly empty. Threads inside each member are possible, but the bookmark is one offset per partition. It can only move past a record once every record before it is done. So you end up tracking finished offsets by hand (Consumers: the poll loop, offsets and delivery guarantees). And a record that keeps failing still blocks its partition (Failure handling: poison pills, retries and dead letters).

What the texting program wants is the queue from Queues and brokers: acknowledgements, redelivery and fan-out. Any free worker takes the next message and acknowledges it, which means it tells the broker it's done. A message whose worker died goes to someone else. Order doesn't matter here: two texts for two payments can go out either way round. In the kitchen, that's any free cook taking the next slip from any rail.

A **share group** gives a topic exactly that. Its members don't own partitions. They all read from all of them, and the broker, the server that runs Kafka, hands out records one by one. So several members read one partition at the same time, and the partition count no longer limits the number of readers. In code, you use `KafkaShareConsumer` instead of `KafkaConsumer`. It has the same `subscribe` and `poll` (the call that fetches the next records). There's no handling of partitions, bookmarks or `seek` (a jump to an offset) of your own. Here it is in Java, then in Kotlin:

```java
try (var consumer = new KafkaShareConsumer<String, String>(Map.of(
        "bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092",
        "group.id", "notify",                               // a share group: no partitions of its own
        "key.deserializer", StringDeserializer.class, "value.deserializer", StringDeserializer.class))) {
    consumer.subscribe(List.of("notifications"));
    while (running) {
        for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> r : consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500)))
            sms.send(r.value());                            // implicit mode: the next poll accepts this batch
    }
}
```

```kotlin
KafkaShareConsumer<String, String>(mapOf(
    "bootstrap.servers" to "localhost:9092",
    "group.id" to "notify",                                 // a share group: no partitions of its own
    "key.deserializer" to StringDeserializer::class.java, "value.deserializer" to StringDeserializer::class.java,
)).use { consumer ->
    consumer.subscribe(listOf("notifications"))
    while (running) {
        for (r in consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500)))
            sms.send(r.value())                             // implicit mode: the next poll accepts this batch
    }
}
```

The topic itself doesn't change. A consumer group and a share group can read it at the same time, each keeping its own progress.

Share groups arrived through KIP-932. (A KIP, a Kafka Improvement Proposal, is the numbered document in which Kafka's developers agree on a change.) They were early access in Kafka 4.0, a preview in 4.1, and production-ready since 4.2. Kafka 4.4 added a dead-letter topic for them, which the next section covers. A share group's progress is kept by a broker job called the share coordinator, in an internal topic of its own. A cluster with fewer than three brokers must lower that topic to one copy (`share.coordinator.state.topic.replication.factor` and `share.coordinator.state.topic.min.isr` set to 1), as the lab broker does.

The Try-it runs both kinds of group on a topic with one partition. Next, we'll see how the broker keeps two members from getting the same record.

