package sfz.msg.l11.demo;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.Admin;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.NewTopic;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.OffsetSpec;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.OffsetAndMetadata;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

/**
 * Consumer lag, computed the way a monitoring tool computes it: for each partition, the log end offset minus the
 * group's committed offset. A settlement group reads 1,000 of 3,000 payments in a 3-partition topic and commits.
 */
public class LagDemo {
    static final String BOOTSTRAP = "localhost:9092";

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        long run = System.currentTimeMillis() % 100_000;
        String topic = "payments-" + run, group = "settlement-" + run;
        try (var admin = Admin.create(Map.<String, Object>of("bootstrap.servers", BOOTSTRAP))) {
            admin.createTopics(List.of(new NewTopic(topic, 3, (short) 1))).all().get();
            try (var p = new KafkaProducer<>(Map.<String, Object>of("bootstrap.servers", BOOTSTRAP), new StringSerializer(), new StringSerializer())) {
                for (int i = 0; i < 3000; i++) p.send(new ProducerRecord<>(topic, "acct-" + i % 30, "pay-" + i));
            }
            try (var c = new KafkaConsumer<>(Map.<String, Object>of("bootstrap.servers", BOOTSTRAP, "group.id", group,
                    "group.protocol", "consumer", "auto.offset.reset", "earliest", "enable.auto.commit", false, "max.poll.records", 100),
                    new StringDeserializer(), new StringDeserializer())) {
                c.subscribe(List.of(topic));
                int read = 0;
                while (read < 1000) read += c.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500)).count();
                c.commitSync();                                    // the group's bookmarks: where each partition got to
                System.out.println("the settlement group read " + read + " payments and committed");
            }
            // what a lag exporter does: two admin calls, then a subtraction per partition
            Map<TopicPartition, OffsetAndMetadata> committed = admin.listConsumerGroupOffsets(group).partitionsToOffsetAndMetadata().get();
            var ends = admin.listOffsets(committed.keySet().stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(tp -> tp, tp -> OffsetSpec.latest()))).all().get();
            long total = 0;
            for (var e : new TreeMap<TopicPartition, OffsetAndMetadata>((a, b) -> a.partition() - b.partition()) {{ putAll(committed); }}.entrySet()) {
                long end = ends.get(e.getKey()).offset(), lag = end - e.getValue().offset();
                total += lag;
                System.out.printf("partition %d: log end %4d, committed %4d, lag %4d%n", e.getKey().partition(), end, e.getValue().offset(), lag);
            }
            System.out.println("total lag " + total + " records");
            System.out.println("now try: kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --describe --group " + group);
        }
    }
}

sections.body:
Every Kafka lesson in this track wrote the read loop by hand: call `poll`, handle the records, save the bookmark, repeat. Kotlin programmers would rather collect a `Flow`, Kotlin's type for values that arrive over time. A coroutine handles them: a light task that can pause without holding up a thread. Two facts from Consumers: the poll loop, offsets and delivery guarantees shape the wrapper.

First, a consumer isn't thread-safe. Every call to it must come from one thread, one line of work inside the program. Second, `poll` blocks that thread for up to its timeout. So the loop runs on a thread of its own and hands each record over to the code that handles it. When that code is slow, the hand-over waits, which slows the polling too. When that code stops, the wrapper calls `wakeup()`, the one consumer method another thread may call. A `poll` that is waiting throws `WakeupException` at once, and the poll thread closes the consumer. Commit on that thread as well, never from the handling code.

Here is the design, first in Java and then in Kotlin. The Java version hands records over through a small `BlockingQueue`, a queue that makes the writer wait when it's full:

```java
Thread pollThread = new Thread(() -> {                       // every consumer call on this one thread
    try {
        while (true) for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> r : consumer.poll(pollTimeout)) handoff.put(r);   // put waits for a slow worker
    } catch (WakeupException e) {
        // the worker is done and wakeup() ended a blocked poll
    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
    } finally {
        consumer.close();
    }
}, "kafka-poll");
pollThread.start();
// ... the worker takes records from handoff (an ArrayBlockingQueue) ...
consumer.wakeup();                                            // the one call another thread may make
pollThread.join();
```

```kotlin
fun <K, V> Consumer<K, V>.records(pollTimeout: Duration): Flow<ConsumerRecord<K, V>> = channelFlow {
    val executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor { Thread(it, "kafka-poll") }
    val consumer = this@records
    launch(executor.asCoroutineDispatcher()) {               // every consumer call on this one thread
        try {
            while (isActive) for (r in consumer.poll(pollTimeout)) send(r)   // send waits for a slow collector
        } catch (e: WakeupException) {
            // the collector is gone and wakeup() ended a blocked poll
        } finally {
            withContext(NonCancellable) { consumer.close() }
        }
    }.invokeOnCompletion { executor.shutdown() }
    awaitClose { consumer.wakeup() }                          // runs when the collector stops: another thread
}
```

In the Java version, `put` waits for a slow worker, and the worker's thread calls `wakeup()` when it's done. In Kotlin, `channelFlow` provides the hand-over and `send` waits for a slow collector. `awaitClose` runs when the collector stops, on another thread, so that's where `wakeup()` goes. The lab builds the Kotlin one and tests it against a consumer whose `poll` blocks for 30 seconds.

In bigger programs, a framework often writes this loop: a library that runs it for you and calls your code for each record. Two common ones for Java are Spring for Apache Kafka and Quarkus. Spring and Quarkus are popular toolkits for building Java programs, and each has a part for Kafka. Each makes the choices this track taught you to make: when to commit, what to do with a record that fails, and whether share groups are supported yet. Each also brings its own Kafka client, the library that talks to the brokers. Spring for Apache Kafka 4.1 brings kafka-clients 4.2.2 and Quarkus 3.40 brings 4.1.1, while the labs use 4.4.0. A feature newer than that client isn't there until the framework upgrades, whatever the brokers run. Brokers accept clients from version 2.1 on, so the mix is safe. Next, we'll see the same ideas in a smaller product.

